## Context

I led a three-person team at Globant for a US insurance company. Before leading this team, I maintained a large travel-booking site that integrated Expedia APIs, including performance improvements.

## Problem

The client wanted a site whose content non-engineers could control, like a CMS, but built purely in Angular.

## Architecture

We designed an API-driven approach: a content manager defined components such as buttons and sections, and the Angular app rendered them dynamically as real Angular components, not raw HTML.

## How the team worked

- **One-on-ones from day one.** The whole team started together, so we agreed standards and rules as a group, not handed down.
- **Quality gates.** I introduced GitHub Actions to enforce code quality and best practices on every change.
- **Real ownership.** Engineers owned whole areas end to end, such as test automation and performance work, not just tickets.
- **Distributed team.** One engineer worked from Argentina, so planning was arranged around time zones.

### A tough moment

One engineer struggled to communicate in client meetings. I used one-on-ones to coach him and got him into Globant's internal training. Over time his questions to the client became clearer and more confident.

## Results

- The client was satisfied at launch, and the site is still live.
- Our most experienced engineer led the shared types and TypeScript standards, and later moved on to a role in Europe.
- Another engineer became a tech lead at Globant.
